.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH wsblast 1  "wsblast 0.2.0" 
.SH NAME
wsblast \- High\-performance WebSocket load\-testing CLI with percentile latency & CI gating
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwsblast\fR [\fB\-u\fR|\fB\-\-url\fR] [\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-\-connections\fR] [\fB\-\-ramp\-rate\fR] [\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-duration\fR] [\fB\-\-stages\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-requests\fR] [\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-\-rate\fR] [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-payload\fR] [\fB\-\-payload\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-binary\fR] [\fB\-H\fR|\fB\-\-header\fR] [\fB\-\-subprotocol\fR] [\fB\-\-mode\fR] [\fB\-\-connect\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-message\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-match\fR] [\fB\-\-ping\-interval\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-p50\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-p95\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-p99\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-p999\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-error\-rate\fR] [\fB\-\-min\-throughput\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-generator\-lag\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-type\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-token\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-token\-cmd\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-token\-cmd\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-token\-refresh\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-header\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-apikey\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-apikey\-header\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-alg\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-key\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-claims\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-ttl\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-kid\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-per\-connection\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-oauth2\-token\-url\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-oauth2\-client\-id\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-oauth2\-client\-secret\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-oauth2\-scope\fR] [\fB\-\-auth\-oauth2\-audience\fR] [\fB\-\-graphql\-ws\-operation\-name\fR] [\fB\-\-graphql\-ws\-operation\fR] [\fB\-\-graphql\-ws\-variables\fR] [\fB\-\-graphql\-ws\-extensions\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR] [\fB\-\-compare\fR] [\fB\-\-compare\-threshold\fR] [\fB\-\-compare\-window\fR] [\fB\-\-fail\-on\-regression\fR] [\fB\-\-capture\fR] [\fB\-\-capture\-limit\fR] [\fB\-\-chaos\-delay\fR] [\fB\-\-chaos\-drop\fR] [\fB\-\-chaos\-corrupt\fR] [\fB\-\-chaos\-close\fR] [\fB\-\-chaos\-close\-mode\fR] [\fB\-\-chaos\-seed\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-tui\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-reconnect\fR] [\fB\-\-prometheus\-listen\fR] [\fB\-\-otel\-endpoint\fR] [\fB\-\-otel\-interval\fR] [\fB\-\-otel\-service\-name\fR] [\fB\-\-scenario\fR] [\fB\-\-phase\fR] [\fB\-\-agents\fR] [\fB\-\-agent\-token\fR] [\fB\-\-agent\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-agent\-ca\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fIURL\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
wsblast stresses WebSocket targets, computes high\-resolution latency percentiles (p50/p95/p99/p99.9), maps failure taxonomy, and gates CI pipelines via SLO thresholds.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-url\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Target WebSocket endpoint flag (alternative to positional argument)
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-connections\fR \fI<NUM>\fR [default: 50]
Number of concurrent WebSocket connection workers
.TP
\fB\-\-ramp\-rate\fR \fI<WORKERS_PER_SEC>\fR [default: 0]
Connection ramp rate in workers per second (0 = spawn all connections immediately)
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-duration\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR [default: 10s]
Total test duration including the connection ramp (e.g., \*(Aq10s\*(Aq, \*(Aq30s\*(Aq, \*(Aq2m\*(Aq, \*(Aq500ms\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-\-stages\fR \fI<STAGES>\fR
Staged load profile instead of \-c, \-d and \-\-ramp\-rate: comma\-separated DURATION:CONNECTIONS stages, each moving the connection count linearly from the previous target (starting at 0), e.g. \*(Aq30s:100,2m:100,30s:0\*(Aq
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-requests\fR \fI<NUM>\fR
Maximum total messages to send across all workers (optional limit)
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-rate\fR \fI<MSG_PER_SEC>\fR [default: 0]
Message rate per connection per second (0 = unthrottled / maximum possible)
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-payload\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
Inline message payload to send (supports {{timestamp}}, {{worker_id}}, {{seq}})
.TP
\fB\-\-payload\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Path to a file containing the message payload
.TP
\fB\-\-binary\fR
Send payload as WebSocket binary frames instead of text frames
.TP
\fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-header\fR \fI<HEADER:VALUE>\fR
Custom HTTP header to include in the WebSocket handshake (can be specified multiple times)
.TP
\fB\-\-subprotocol\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Optional WebSocket subprotocol to request during handshake
.TP
\fB\-\-mode\fR \fI<MODE>\fR [default: echo]
Load testing execution mode
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
echo: Send request frame, await response, and record round\-trip latency (RTT)
.IP \(bu 2
stream: Continuously stream frames without awaiting per\-frame acknowledgments
.IP \(bu 2
listen: Connect workers and passively record incoming server\-pushed broadcast messages
.IP \(bu 2
graphql\-ws: Apollo GraphQL WS protocol (subscriptions)
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-connect\-timeout\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR [default: 5s]
Connection handshake timeout (e.g., \*(Aq5s\*(Aq, \*(Aq2000ms\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-\-message\-timeout\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR [default: 5s]
Per\-message response timeout in echo mode (e.g., \*(Aq5s\*(Aq, \*(Aq1000ms\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-\-match\fR \fI<MODE>\fR [default: first]
Echo reply matching: first (default: the first data frame after a send is the reply), exact, or json:POINTER (an RFC 6901 pointer such as json:/seq)

first: the first text or binary frame received after a send is taken as the reply, even when it is an unrelated server push such as a broadcast or heartbeat, which then records a wrong RTT.

exact: the reply must be byte\-identical to the frame that was sent.

json:POINTER: the reply is parsed as JSON and the value at the RFC 6901 pointer (e.g. json:/seq) must equal this request\*(Aqs {{seq}}, as a number or a numeric string. The payload must contain {{seq}}.

With exact and json:POINTER, other data frames are counted as unsolicited and the worker keeps waiting for its reply until \-\-message\-timeout.
.TP
\fB\-\-ping\-interval\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR [default: 0s]
Periodic ping heartbeat interval (e.g., \*(Aq10s\*(Aq, \*(Aq0s\*(Aq to disable)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-p50\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR
SLO threshold: Maximum acceptable p50 round\-trip latency (e.g., \*(Aq10ms\*(Aq, \*(Aq500us\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-p95\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR
SLO threshold: Maximum acceptable p95 round\-trip latency (e.g., \*(Aq50ms\*(Aq, \*(Aq20ms\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-p99\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR
SLO threshold: Maximum acceptable p99 round\-trip latency (e.g., \*(Aq100ms\*(Aq, \*(Aq50ms\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-p999\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR
SLO threshold: Maximum acceptable p99.9 round\-trip latency (e.g., \*(Aq250ms\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-error\-rate\fR \fI<FLOAT>\fR
SLO threshold: Maximum acceptable error rate fraction (e.g., \*(Aq0.01\*(Aq for 1%, \*(Aq0.001\*(Aq for 0.1%)
.TP
\fB\-\-min\-throughput\fR \fI<RPS>\fR
SLO threshold: Minimum required message throughput (messages/sec)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-generator\-lag\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR
SLO threshold: Maximum p99 wake\-up lag of the load generator itself (e.g. \*(Aq20ms\*(Aq); a breach means the latency numbers include client\-side delay
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-type\fR \fI<TYPE>\fR [default: none]
Authentication type
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
none: No authentication
.IP \(bu 2
static: Static bearer token
.IP \(bu 2
apikey: API Key
.IP \(bu 2
jwt: JWT signed by wsblast with the \-\-auth\-jwt\-* options (needs the jwt feature)
.IP \(bu 2
oauth2: OAuth2 client\-credentials token from the \-\-auth\-oauth2\-* options (needs the oauth2 feature)
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-token\fR \fI<TOKEN>\fR
Static token for auth (used with \-\-auth\-type static)
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-token\-cmd\fR \fI<COMMAND>\fR
Command whose trimmed stdout is the token or API key, run through the platform shell before the run (replaces \-\-auth\-token or \-\-auth\-apikey)
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-token\-cmd\-timeout\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR
Time allowed for each run of \-\-auth\-token\-cmd [default: 30s]
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-token\-refresh\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR
Re\-run \-\-auth\-token\-cmd every DURATION during the run; new handshakes use the latest token
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-header\fR \fI<HEADER>\fR [default: Authorization]
Header that carries the token (used with every \-\-auth\-type except apikey)
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-apikey\fR \fI<KEY>\fR
API Key (used with \-\-auth\-type apikey)
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-apikey\-header\fR \fI<HEADER>\fR [default: X\-API\-Key]
API Key header name (used with \-\-auth\-type apikey)
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-alg\fR \fI<ALG>\fR
JWT signing algorithm (used with \-\-auth\-type jwt) [default: HS256]
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
HS256: HMAC with SHA\-256
.IP \(bu 2
HS384: HMAC with SHA\-384
.IP \(bu 2
HS512: HMAC with SHA\-512
.IP \(bu 2
RS256: RSASSA\-PKCS1\-v1_5 with SHA\-256
.IP \(bu 2
ES256: ECDSA with P\-256 and SHA\-256
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-key\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
File with the JWT signing key: the secret bytes for HS*, a PEM private key for RS256 and ES256; or set WSBLAST_JWT_KEY_FILE
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-claims\fR \fI<JSON>\fR
JSON object of claims added to every JWT (e.g. \*(Aq{"sub":"loadtest","aud":"ws"}\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-ttl\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR
JWT lifetime, which sets iat, nbf and exp; tokens are re\-signed before they expire [default: 5m]
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-kid\fR \fI<KEY_ID>\fR
Key ID put in the JWT header as kid, for servers that look the verification key up in a JWKS
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-jwt\-per\-connection\fR
Sign a fresh JWT with a unique jti for every connection attempt instead of sharing one token
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-oauth2\-token\-url\fR \fI<URL>\fR
OAuth2 token endpoint for the client\-credentials grant (used with \-\-auth\-type oauth2)
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-oauth2\-client\-id\fR \fI<ID>\fR
OAuth2 client id (used with \-\-auth\-type oauth2)
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-oauth2\-client\-secret\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
File with the OAuth2 client secret; or set WSBLAST_OAUTH2_CLIENT_SECRET
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-oauth2\-scope\fR \fI<SCOPE>\fR
Scope requested with the OAuth2 token (space\-separated)
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-oauth2\-audience\fR \fI<AUDIENCE>\fR
Audience parameter sent with the OAuth2 token request, for servers that require one
.TP
\fB\-\-graphql\-ws\-operation\-name\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
GraphQL WS operation name
.TP
\fB\-\-graphql\-ws\-operation\fR \fI<OPERATION>\fR
GraphQL WS operation (query/mutation/subscription)
.TP
\fB\-\-graphql\-ws\-variables\fR \fI<JSON>\fR
GraphQL WS variables (JSON)
.TP
\fB\-\-graphql\-ws\-extensions\fR \fI<JSON>\fR
GraphQL WS extensions (JSON)
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR [default: text]
Output report format
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
text: Human\-readable styled terminal tables
.IP \(bu 2
json: Schema\-versioned JSON for CI parsing and metric archiving
.IP \(bu 2
markdown: GitHub Flavored Markdown table for PR comments and job summaries
.IP \(bu 2
html: A self\-contained HTML page with charts of the time series, to open in a browser or attach to a CI run
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-compare\fR \fI<FILE>...\fR
Compare two JSON reports for regression detection (\-\-format text, json, markdown or html)
.TP
\fB\-\-compare\-threshold\fR \fI<PERCENT>\fR [default: 10]
Regression threshold percentage (default: 10%)
.TP
\fB\-\-compare\-window\fR \fI<START..END>\fR
Restrict the time\-series metrics of \-\-compare to START..END of each run (e.g., \*(Aq10s..50s\*(Aq, \*(Aq10s..\*(Aq, \*(Aq..50s\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-regression\fR
Fail on regression (exit code 2)
.TP
\fB\-\-capture\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Record every data frame and connection event to FILE as JSON lines (see METHODOLOGY.md)
.TP
\fB\-\-capture\-limit\fR \fI<N>\fR [default: 100000]
Maximum records \-\-capture writes; later ones are dropped and counted (0 = unlimited)
.TP
\fB\-\-chaos\-delay\fR \fI<DURATION[:JITTER]>\fR
Chaos: pause DURATION, plus up to JITTER drawn uniformly, before every data send (e.g. \*(Aq5ms\*(Aq or \*(Aq5ms:20ms\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-\-chaos\-drop\fR \fI<PERCENT>\fR
Chaos: skip this percentage (0 to 100) of data sends; they are counted as dropped, not as sent
.TP
\fB\-\-chaos\-corrupt\fR \fI<PERCENT>\fR
Chaos: change one byte of the payload of this percentage (0 to 100) of data sends; echo replies are still matched and timed
.TP
\fB\-\-chaos\-close\fR \fI<PERCENT>\fR
Chaos: close the connection after this percentage (0 to 100) of replies, or of sends in stream mode; the worker reconnects, and closes are not errors
.TP
\fB\-\-chaos\-close\-mode\fR \fI<MODE>\fR [default: abrupt]
How \-\-chaos\-close ends a connection
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
abrupt: Closes the TCP connection without a WebSocket Close frame
.IP \(bu 2
graceful: Sends a Close frame with code 1001 (going away), then closes the TCP connection without waiting for the server\*(Aqs Close frame
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-chaos\-seed\fR \fI<N>\fR
Chaos: seed of every chaos decision, to repeat a run\*(Aqs faults [default: picked per run and shown in the report]
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Write output report to the specified file path
.TP
\fB\-\-tui\fR
Launch interactive terminal dashboard (TUI)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR
Suppress interactive progress bar (recommended for non\-interactive CI/CD runners)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-reconnect\fR
End a connection\*(Aqs worker after a timeout, I/O error or server close instead of reconnecting with backoff
.TP
\fB\-\-prometheus\-listen\fR \fI<ADDR>\fR
Serve live metrics at http://ADDR/metrics in the Prometheus text format while the run lasts (e.g. \*(Aq127.0.0.1:9464\*(Aq; port 0 picks a free port)
.TP
\fB\-\-otel\-endpoint\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Export live metrics over OTLP/HTTP with protobuf bodies to this base URL, e.g. \*(Aqhttp://localhost:4318\*(Aq (needs the otel feature)
.TP
\fB\-\-otel\-interval\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR [default: 10s]
How often metrics are exported over OTLP; the final values are also exported when the run ends
.TP
\fB\-\-otel\-service\-name\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
OpenTelemetry service.name resource attribute [default: $OTEL_SERVICE_NAME, else wsblast]
.TP
\fB\-\-scenario\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Path to scenario YAML file defining multi\-phase test (conflicts with individual flags)
.TP
\fB\-\-phase\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Run only a specific phase from the scenario by name
.TP
\fB\-\-agents\fR \fI<HOST:PORT>\fR
Generate the load on these agents (started with `wsblast agent`) instead of locally; comma\-separated HOST:PORT, ws://HOST:PORT or wss://HOST:PORT
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\-token\fR \fI<TOKEN>\fR
Token the agents were started with [default: $WSBLAST_AGENT_TOKEN]
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\-timeout\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR [default: 30s]
Time the agents get to become ready before the start, and to send their results after the end of the run
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\-ca\fR \fI<PEM>\fR
PEM file of CA certificates to trust for wss:// agents, in addition to the platform\*(Aqs roots
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
[\fIURL\fR]
Target WebSocket URL (e.g., ws://127.0.0.1:9001/ws or wss://api.example.com/stream)
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
wsblast\-mock(1)
Run a local WebSocket test server: echo with optional delay, jitter, stalls, rejections and closes, or replay of a capture file
.TP
wsblast\-agent(1)
Run a load agent that generates load for a coordinator (`wsblast URL \-\-agents ...`), one test at a time
.TP
wsblast\-render(1)
Render a saved JSON report (from \-\-format json) or comparison (from \-\-compare \-\-format json) as HTML, Markdown or text without running a test
.SH VERSION
v0.2.0
